cy were  fortunate of a very progressive water  agency for the nation's singapore pub  has already  begun to look at ways to enhance the  treatment process  and i like this last statement they note  that at current  screening technologies really do not  allow  for the detection of trace amounts in  this case of an  organic chemical tetrahydrofuran which  was responsible  for that odor i mentioned before so  what we realize is that our water  systems are susceptible  and regardless of of how advanced the  treatment may be  there are always be certain chemicals  that are able to evade the system  and we have to think more holistically  about the health impacts if any  and how we can better detect and deal  with  emerging contaminants  so i was very impressed when this thf  spill happened in malaysia and detected  in singapore it's interesting that the  first  detector was not an instrument it was  people's nose so the human nose  is still hard to beat but regardless  very quickly singapore pub was able to  utilize their analytical capabilities  and determine the the chemical as being  tetrahydrofuran or thf so i think  even though online sensors may not be  able to immediately detect a chemical  such as thf  we do have the tools to very quickly  identify  almost anything that you can imagine in  the water supply  but this begs the bigger question are we  looking for the right things are we  really  understanding what has the most impacts  to our treatment systems to  public health and are important for our  monitoring systems  so what is it we should monitor for and  i've spent a lot of time on various  expert panels both for the for the  federal government  us epa and especially in california  around their water reuse and this  topic goes round and round even with  the time i've spent working with the  world health organization  it's very difficult to say what are the  most  important substances to monitor  so just think about it if we have say an  effluent be it ind